export const G_QUESTION =
  "Before we go out to raise, what should we fix first, and which of the numbers we quote are actually backed by our management accounts?";

/** Q's stored reply to G (run a4b412eb), word for word. */
export const G_REPLY =
  "Fix the evidence bridge first: reconcile the headline traction and financial metrics to the underlying records, then tighten the security-outcome and financing evidence. Investors will otherwise see a credible product with numbers that do not yet form one consistent case.\n\n1. The underlying evidence does not yet cleanly bridge those figures to invoices, monthly revenue and customer records. Make the distinction between licences, enrolled staff, active users and ARR explicit, customer by customer and month by month. The invoice register supports individual licence invoices and deployment fees, but the extract available here does not establish the September totals.\n\n2. **Reconcile the financials before circulating the deck.** Your management accounts are documented as showing 29.3% gross margin, £92,340 net burn and 4.2 months of runway for the 12 months to October 2026. The deck shows 82% gross margin, about £70,000 monthly net burn and 6.9 months of runway at 30 September 2026. The accounts separately show 82.0% licence gross margin when deployment costs are excluded. These may be different bases, but the documents do not yet explain the differences sufficiently. Put the calculation, cost allocation, period and cash bridge beside each headline number.\n\n3. **Strengthen the evidence behind the customer and security claims.** The phishing outcome method explains that the zero-takeover measure uses customer security logs and identity-provider records for 64,000 enrolled accounts, and the board minutes note the same outcome. That supports the reporting method and company claim, but it is not independent verification. The customer evidence also needs care: the four-customer summary is labelled a synthetic demo document, so it should not carry the weight of primary customer proof.\n\n4. **Make the raise case internally consistent.** The deck asks for £3m at a £10m pre-money valuation, with a 16 December 2026 target close, and gives a post-raise burn and runway. Tie those assumptions directly to the hiring plan, use of funds and cash-flow model. The current materials establish the planned hiring lines, but not a fully reconciled financing bridge.\n\nThe numbers that are actually backed by management-account evidence are the management-account figures themselves: 29.3% gross margin, £92,340 net burn and 4.2 months of runway for the 12 months to October 2026, plus the separate 82.0% licence gross-margin calculation excluding deployment costs. They are document-supported company claims, not independently verified. The deck’s 82% margin, roughly £70k burn and 6.9-month runway are also company-reported, but they are not matched to the management-account basis. The deck’s £852k ARR, 71,000 licensed seats, 64,000 enrolled staff and 11 customers are not cleanly substantiated by the management-account evidence currently established here. The £3m raise terms and current cap table have the strongest documentary trail, but they are not management-account figures.\n\nMy recommendation is to pause broad outreach until you issue a reconciled one-page KPI bridge. That is the highest-leverage fix: it addresses the main contradiction without changing the underlying story. I would change that view if the management accounts contain a clear reconciliation showing that the different margins, burn rates, runway figures and traction totals use deliberately different definitions and periods.";

